# 50 MHz on the CORE clock (period 20.000000 ns) -- hardware clock, and the
# frequency this build is allowed to be reported at.
#
# Hardware chain: 27 MHz on D18 -> GTP_PPLL I=1/M=1/F=50/O0=27 -> 27 x 50/27 = 50.000 MHz.
# VCO = 27 x 50 = 1350 MHz (legal 1330..2133), PFD 27 MHz.
#
# P48e: single clock, no over-constraint clock.  This file used to carry an
# extra `core_clk_pnr` at 52 MHz "to make the placer try harder".  The measured
# evidence says that backfires:
#
#   build                     tightest declared clock   routed path
#   p48r48d (48 MHz claim)    20.000 ns (pnr 50)       19.355 ns   <- best ever
#   p48r50  (50 MHz claim)    19.231 ns (pnr 52)       21.600 ns
#   p48r50b (50 MHz claim)    19.231 ns (pnr 52)       21.120 ns
#
# So the *looser* 20.000 ns target places AND routes better here, and 20.000 ns
# is exactly the 50 MHz period.  Declaring the 50 MHz claim with a single clock
# at 20.000 ns therefore reproduces the placement pressure that gave the best
# routed result ever measured on this netlist, while the claim row is the same
# 20.000 ns the hardware actually runs at.
create_clock -name board_clk -period 37.037037 -waveform {0 18.5185185} [get_ports {clk}]
create_clock -name core_clk -period 20.000000 -waveform {0 10.000000} [get_pins {u_core_pll/CLKOUT0}]
